o  the public when it went off the air  landing a deal with hewlett-packard to  have them all uploaded to YouTube for  fans to watch ad free coupling the  office may be the model example of a  British TV series turning into a  successful American sitcom but many  attempts to take comedy stateside have  failed one such experiment was coupling  NBC's 2003 attempt at a DAB  the bbc2 romantic comedy series from  Doctor Who's Steven Moffat the show is  based on his relationship with producer  sue virtue NBC started looking into the  show when friends was nearing the end of  its run however their attempts to bring  coupling to the states were troubled  from the start with the network's  shooting and then scrapping a pilot  because they felt Moffat script was too  confusing three of the six lead roles  were recast when the pilot was reshot  want us to split up yes  coupling ended up getting the axe after  only four low rated episodes on NBC  although Moffat had much more success  with the British version which ran for  four series Moffat blamed NBC for the  show's cancellation and later said I  learned not to work in America I say  that slightly facetiously but truthfully  I'm British what is the point of me  going to try to make it somewhere else  Ironside remakes may be all the rage  nowadays but not all of them are  successful such was the case with NBC's  2013 attempt at remaking the popular 60s  and 70s cop procedural Ironside this  time with blair Underwood starring as  the title character a wheelchair-bound  police officer who solved the city's  most difficult cases just say that a  different view of the world from down  here  Underwood's take on the show was  imagined as grittier and darker than the  original which didn't sit well with  viewers the show was also hit with  controversy from disability rights  advocates with some groups calling out  NBC for casting a non-disabled actor in  the role of a disabled character the  show's producers claimed they needed an  